<reference_puzzle index="11" source="eval" puzzle_id="e87109e9" lines="68-83">
<description>
Emit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.

Input structure:
- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.
- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.

Rule:
- Remove the legend from the output.
- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).
- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.
- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.
- Beams stop at grid edges.

Key insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="eval" puzzle_id="a395ee82" lines="9-24">
<description>
Stamp translated copies of a monochrome template guided by a singleton pixel layout.

Input structure:
- bg = background. A large monochrome shape (template, color T).
- Singleton pixels elsewhere: exactly one is color T (layout origin); the rest share color O (overlap color).
- Singletons sit on a uniform-spacing lattice.

Rule:
- Convert each singleton's offset from the origin into coarse lattice units.
- Place a template copy at each offset, scaled by template height/width.
- The origin's copy is painted O instead of T.
- Any pixel covered by two or more copies is also recolored to O.

Key insight: The single T-colored singleton identifies both the origin position and the "self" copy, which is uniquely recolored.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="13" source="eval" puzzle_id="8698868d" lines="9-23">
<description>
Match sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.

Input structure:
- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.
- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).

Rule:
- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).
- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.
- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.
- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.

Key insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="14" source="eval" puzzle_id="88e364bc" lines="102-115">
<description>
Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.
- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.

Rule:
- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.
- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.
- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.

Key insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

